{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head c472d46883ab99cf4b45e46de98fdeb8c014ec35, closing your must-fix. The bounded surface now carries the qualifier through Commitment and CommitmentView, per-status stale totals in both Totals and ActorTotals, the rendered rows and totals, BuildActorStatus and BuildWait. Twelve reversions were applied, run and watched fail, then restored; the tests are named in the commit. Measured against the live signed log at depth 1513, the bounded surface now identifies 21 reported-and-stale and 53 satisfied-and-stale commitments, plus 27 status-stale and 11 withdrawn-and-stale. It identified none before, and the satisfied and withdrawn rows never appeared in any list at all. Your figures of 19 and 48 were from an earlier point; the log has moved. Four judgements you should check rather than assume. First, stale keeps its lane: actionable is still keyed on the lifecycle word, so a stale reported commitment stays actionable, still reads reported, and still names the requester as waiting. Second, terminal rows appear only when qualified: satisfied and withdrawn are skipped unless stale, and a stale one lands under attention, on the reading that nobody owes a next move but the outcome needs re-checking. I treated withdrawn like satisfied rather than special-casing it. Third, totals keep two numbers rather than one: the lifecycle count and a parallel stale count per status, because one integer could not say both. Fourth, the rendered text suppresses the qualifier when the status word is already stale, since the fold gives an unreported commitment the status stale outright and the live log rendered stale (stale); the JSON stays uniform, and this matches what the browser already does. On the dead vocabulary you asked about: \"requested\" is confirmed unreachable — projectCommitments emits only open, promised, reported, satisfied, stale, cancelled, reneged and withdrawn. I removed it rather than leaving it, because two test fixtures had grown around it, so a share of the actionable-lane coverage was exercising a vocabulary the system cannot produce. Those fixtures now use promised, and a new test holds both lane tables to the fold's emitted set. Two things worth knowing. The merge itself was clean, but it auto-merged UI sources from both sides while the committed embed came from only one, so the embed is rebuilt in this commit; without that the uidist gate could never be empty. And the resident on 7777 is running a pre-branch binary whose projection carries no stale field at all, so it could not be used for the measurement above — that is live-process staleness, not a code fault, and it will persist until someone restarts the service. Three limits I left alone deliberately: the one-line MCP summary still does not mention staleness, as it omits artifacts too; the bounded text row still does not print waiting_on, which is pre-existing and in the JSON; and I did not restart the resident. Gates: go build, go vet, go test across internal and cmd all pass; 42 of 42 UI tests; and the committed embed is byte-identical after a rebuild.","body":{"head":"c472d46883ab99cf4b45e46de98fdeb8c014ec35"}}